-- Q2456: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All contracts regardless of their subject matter","Contracts performable within one year of formation","Contracts for sale of goods priced at $500 or more","Oral contracts made between close family members"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under UCC Section 2-201, contracts for the sale of goods priced at $500 or more must be evidenced by a writing signed by the party to be charged. The Statute of Frauds also covers contracts for the sale of land, contracts not performable within one year, suretyship promises, and marriage contracts.
Wrong (A): The Statute of Frauds applies only to specific categories of contracts, not all contracts regardless of subject matter.
Wrong (B): Contracts that can be performed within one year do not require a writing; the statute applies only to those that cannot be performed within one year.
Wrong (D): There is no Statute of Frauds exception or requirement based on familial relationships between contracting parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2456;

-- Q2466: Business Law: Contracts [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The contract was a bad financial bargain for one party","One party''s financial circumstances changed after signing","The contract was entered into under duress or coercion","One party failed to read the contract before signing"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts, Section 175, duress—including threats of physical harm, wrongful detention of property, or improper economic pressure—vitiates genuine assent and renders a contract voidable. Fraud and misrepresentation under Section 164 also provide valid defenses based on lack of genuine assent.
Wrong (A): A bad bargain alone is not a defense to contract enforcement; courts generally do not rescue parties from unfavorable deals absent fraud or unconscionability.
Wrong (B): Changed financial circumstances after formation do not void a contract; only conditions existing at formation affect assent.
Wrong (D): Failure to read a contract before signing is generally not a valid defense; parties are bound by terms they had the opportunity to review.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2466;

-- Q2470: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$5,000, equal to the full contract price","$500, representing 10% of total contract value","Nothing, because Martin was able to find substitute goods","$1,000, the cover-price-to-contract-price difference times quantity"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under UCC Section 2-712, when a seller breaches, the buyer may cover by making a reasonable substitute purchase and recover the difference between the cover price and the contract price. Here, cover damages are ($12 - $10) x 500 = $1,000, plus any incidental or consequential damages.
Wrong (A): The full contract price of $5,000 is not the correct measure; cover damages are the difference between cover and contract price, not the entire price.
Wrong (B): There is no 10% formula for calculating cover damages under the UCC.
Wrong (C): The ability to cover does not eliminate the right to damages—it establishes the measure of recovery.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2470;

-- Q2471: Business Law: Contracts [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, the acceptance was effective upon dispatch under the mailbox rule","No, because Parker revoked the offer before Quinn could accept it","No, because the revocation was mailed before the acceptance was sent","Yes, but only if Quinn knew of the revocation before accepting"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts, Section 63 (the mailbox rule), an acceptance is effective when properly dispatched. A revocation under Section 42 is effective only when received by the offeree. Quinn dispatched her acceptance Thursday before receiving the revocation, so a valid contract was formed.
Wrong (B): Parker''s revocation was not effective when mailed—it required actual receipt by Quinn, which had not yet occurred when Quinn accepted.
Wrong (C): The timing of mailing the revocation is irrelevant; revocations are effective upon receipt, not dispatch, unlike acceptances.
Wrong (D): Quinn''s awareness of the revocation is relevant only if she received it before dispatching her acceptance, which did not happen here.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2471;

-- Q2475: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Performance time has not expired, or the seller reasonably believed tender was acceptable","The buyer has already begun using the delivered goods","More than 30 days have passed since the original delivery date","The buyer expressly accepts the nonconforming goods as delivered"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under UCC Section 2-508, a seller may cure a nonconforming tender in two situations: (1) if the time for performance has not yet expired, the seller may make a conforming delivery within the contract time; or (2) if the seller had reasonable grounds to believe the tender would be acceptable, the seller has additional reasonable time to cure.
Wrong (B): The buyer''s use of goods does not create a right to cure; cure is a seller''s remedy tied to the tender, not the buyer''s use.
Wrong (C): UCC Section 2-508 imposes no 30-day time limit; the right to cure depends on the contract timeline and reasonable expectations.
Wrong (D): Express acceptance of nonconforming goods waives rejection rights, making the cure issue moot.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2475;

-- Q2477: Business Law: Contracts [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Anticipatory repudiation, allowing immediate breach remedies","A minor breach entitling the homeowner to damages only","A condition subsequent that automatically terminates the contract","Impossibility of performance that excuses the painter''s obligation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Second) of Contracts Section 253, anticipatory repudiation occurs when one party unequivocally communicates before the performance date that they will not perform. The non-breaching party may immediately treat the contract as breached and pursue remedies without waiting for the performance deadline.
Wrong (B): A minor breach involves defective but substantial performance; here the painter stated he will not perform at all, which is a total repudiation.
Wrong (C): A condition subsequent is a contractual event that discharges an existing duty; a voluntary refusal to perform is not a condition subsequent.
Wrong (D): Impossibility requires an unforeseen event making performance objectively impossible; the painter simply chose not to perform.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2477;

-- Q2484: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is authorized to conduct a broad range of ongoing transactions","Is appointed only for a single, specific transaction or task","Has no authority to bind the principal in any contracts","Must always be a salaried employee of the principal"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency, Section 2.01, a general agent is authorized to conduct a series of transactions involving a continuity of service for the principal, such as managing an entire business operation on an ongoing basis.
Wrong (B): An agent appointed for a single transaction is a special agent, not a general agent; this describes the narrower scope that distinguishes special agents.
Wrong (C): Both general and special agents can bind the principal in contracts within the scope of their authority.
Wrong (D): A general agent may be an independent contractor; there is no requirement that they be an employee of the principal.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2484;

-- Q2497: Agency and Regulation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Keep accurate records of all funds received and not commingle them","Prepare audited financial statements for the principal annually","File annual federal and state tax returns for the principal","Provide monthly profit and loss statements to the principal"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.12, an agent''s duty of accounting requires maintaining accurate records of all property and funds received or expended on the principal''s behalf. The agent must keep principal funds separate and not commingle them with the agent''s own property.
Wrong (B): Preparing audited financial statements is a function of an independent auditor, not a standard duty of an agent under the accounting obligation.
Wrong (C): Filing tax returns is a separate professional service, not part of the agent''s duty of accounting under agency law.
Wrong (D): The duty of accounting requires accurate recordkeeping, not the preparation of formal profit and loss statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2497;

-- Q2504: Agency and Regulation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Pay the agent a performance bonus for exceptional results","Hold the agent harmless from all liability regardless of conduct","Provide health insurance and retirement benefits to the agent","Reimburse expenses reasonably incurred within the scope of authority"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under the Restatement (Third) of Agency Section 8.14, a principal has a duty to indemnify the agent for expenses and losses reasonably incurred while carrying out authorized duties. Rivera''s $4,200 in documented travel expenses were incurred within the scope of authority, so Beacon must reimburse them.
Wrong (A): The duty of indemnification covers reasonable expenses, not discretionary bonuses for performance.
Wrong (B): Indemnification does not extend to losses caused by the agent''s own negligence or unauthorized conduct; it is limited to authorized acts.
Wrong (C): Employment benefits such as health insurance and retirement plans are separate from the agency duty of indemnification.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2504;

-- Q2515: Business Structures [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A partnership where no partner has personal liability for any obligation","A partnership that is limited to no more than 100 total partners","A partnership that must elect to be taxed as a corporation","A partnership shielding partners from liability for others'' malpractice"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RUPA Section 306(c) and state LLP statutes, an LLP provides partners with a liability shield against vicarious liability for other partners'' negligence, malpractice, or misconduct. Each partner remains fully liable for their own acts and those they directly supervise.
Wrong (A): LLP partners are not shielded from all obligations; they remain personally liable for their own acts, supervisory liability, and partnership debts they personally guarantee.
Wrong (B): There is no statutory cap of 100 partners for LLPs; that limitation applies to S corporations under IRC Section 1361(b)(1)(A).
Wrong (C): An LLP retains pass-through partnership taxation; there is no requirement to elect corporate tax treatment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2515;

-- Q2520: Business Structures [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The corporation has fewer than 10 shareholders on record","The corporation operated only a single line of business","The corporation had elected S corporation tax status","The corporation failed to observe formalities and was an alter ego"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under RMBCA Section 6.22 and the alter ego doctrine, courts pierce the corporate veil when the shareholder failed to observe corporate formalities and used the corporation as a mere instrumentality. Commingling personal and corporate funds is a primary factor demonstrating the corporation is the shareholder''s alter ego.
Wrong (A): Having few shareholders is common in closely held corporations but is not itself a factor supporting veil piercing.
Wrong (B): Operating a single line of business is a legitimate corporate structure and does not indicate abuse of the corporate form.
Wrong (C): S corporation election is a tax classification choice and has no bearing on whether the corporate veil should be pierced.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2520;

-- Q2538: Property Transactions: Basis [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A recognized gain of $10,000 on the sale","A recognized gain of $15,000 on the sale","A recognized loss of $10,000 on the sale","No gain or loss is recognized on the sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1015(a) and Treas. Reg. Section 1.1015-1(a)(2), when gift property has a FMV below the donor''s basis, a dual-basis rule applies. The gain basis is $80,000 (donor''s basis) and the loss basis is $55,000 (FMV at gift date). Because the $70,000 selling price falls between the two bases, no gain or loss is recognized.
Wrong (A): A $10,000 gain would require using the FMV loss basis of $55,000 plus $10,000, but gain is measured from the $80,000 gain basis, and $70,000 is below that amount.
Wrong (B): A $15,000 gain incorrectly uses the $55,000 FMV as the sole basis; the gain basis is the donor''s $80,000 adjusted basis.
Wrong (C): A $10,000 loss would apply only if the sale price were below the $55,000 loss basis; at $70,000 the sale exceeds the loss basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2538;

-- Q2555: Property Transactions: Basis [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Stocks held in a brokerage account at death","Real estate that was owned by the decedent","Income in respect of a decedent items like unpaid salary","A personal automobile owned by the decedent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 1014(c), income in respect of a decedent (IRD) does not receive a stepped-up basis at death. IRD items—such as unpaid wages, accrued interest, IRA distributions, and installment sale receivables—retain their tax character and are taxed to the recipient when received.
Wrong (A): Publicly traded stocks receive a stepped-up basis to FMV at the date of death under Section 1014(a).
Wrong (B): Real estate owned by the decedent is eligible for a stepped-up basis to FMV at death under Section 1014(a).
Wrong (D): A personal automobile is tangible personal property eligible for a stepped-up basis at death under Section 1014(a).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2555;

-- Q2567: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$7,000 net short-term capital loss, fully deductible this year","$7,000 net loss, $3,000 deductible now and $4,000 carried forward","$3,000 ordinary income deduction with no carryforward allowed","$7,000 reclassified as a long-term capital loss for the year"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 1211(b), individual taxpayers may deduct up to $3,000 of net capital losses against ordinary income per year. After netting $8,000 in short-term gains against $15,000 in short-term losses, the taxpayer has a $7,000 net loss. Only $3,000 offsets ordinary income; the remaining $4,000 carries forward under Sec. 1212(b), retaining its short-term character.
Wrong (A): Capital losses are not fully deductible; Section 1211(b) caps the annual deduction at $3,000 for individuals.
Wrong (C): While the $3,000 deduction amount is correct, the excess $4,000 carries forward indefinitely—it is not lost.
Wrong (D): Short-term capital losses do not convert to long-term; they retain their short-term character when carried forward.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2567;

-- Q2580: Property Transactions: Gains/Losses [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Short-term and long-term are combined into one category first","Only long-term transactions are netted; short-term are ordinary","Losses are deducted first and then gains are added back","Each holding period is netted separately, then combined together"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1222, capital gains and losses are netted by holding period: first net short-term gains against short-term losses, then net long-term gains against long-term losses, and finally combine the two net amounts. This ordering matters because short-term gains are taxed at ordinary rates while long-term gains receive preferential rates under Section 1(h).
Wrong (A): Short-term and long-term transactions are not combined into one category; they must be netted separately by holding period first.
Wrong (B): Short-term capital transactions are capital in nature, not ordinary; they follow the same netting process as long-term transactions.
Wrong (C): There is no rule requiring losses to be deducted before gains are added; gains and losses within each holding period are netted simultaneously.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2580;

-- Q2627: Individual Taxation: Income [easy/L1]
