-- Q2628: Individual Taxation: Income [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$20,000 of cancellation of indebtedness income, fully taxable","$50,000 of income based on the original outstanding balance","$30,000 of income based on the settlement amount paid","No income because debt settlements are generally nontaxable"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 61(a)(11), cancellation of indebtedness (COD) income is included in gross income. Victor''s $20,000 of forgiven debt ($50,000 minus $30,000) is taxable because he is neither bankrupt nor insolvent, so the Section 108 exclusions do not apply.
Wrong (B): The taxable amount is the forgiven portion ($20,000), not the entire original $50,000 obligation.
Wrong (C): The $30,000 settlement payment is not income; income arises from the $20,000 that was forgiven, not the amount actually paid.
Wrong (D): Debt settlements are generally taxable under Section 61(a)(11); exclusions under Section 108 apply only in bankruptcy, insolvency, or other narrow circumstances.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2628;

-- Q2639: Individual Taxation: Income [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$12,000, calculated as 50% of the total benefits received","$17,100, based on a single-tier computation of the excess","$20,400, calculated as 85% of the total benefits received","The lesser of the two-tier formula result or 85% of benefits"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 86(a), the taxable amount of Social Security benefits is the lesser of the two-tier formula result or 85% of total benefits. With provisional income of $36,000, the formula yields 50% of excess over $25,000 up to $34,000 ($4,500) plus 85% of excess over $34,000 ($1,700), totaling $6,200—which is less than 85% of benefits ($20,400).
Wrong (A): The 50% calculation applies only to the first tier and does not account for the second tier when provisional income exceeds $34,000.
Wrong (B): $17,100 does not correspond to either tier of the Section 86 formula for this taxpayer''s provisional income.
Wrong (C): While 85% of benefits ($20,400) is computed as a ceiling, the actual taxable amount is the lesser of this ceiling and the two-tier formula result.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2639;

-- Q2640: Individual Taxation: Income [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["All amounts are taxable, but tips under $20/month need not be reported to employer","All tips are excluded from gross income regardless of amount","Wages are taxable only to the extent they exceed the standard deduction","Salaries and wages are taxable but year-end bonuses are excluded"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 61, all wages, salaries, bonuses, and tips are included in gross income. However, employees are not required to report tips to their employer if total tips from that employer are less than $20 in a calendar month, though the income remains taxable on the individual''s return.
Wrong (B): All tips are taxable under Section 61 regardless of amount; there is no blanket exclusion for tip income.
Wrong (C): Wages are fully includible in gross income when received; the standard deduction reduces taxable income but does not exclude wages from gross income.
Wrong (D): Year-end bonuses are compensation and are fully includible in gross income under Section 61, just like regular wages.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2640;

-- Q2652: Individual Taxation: Deductions [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The itemized deduction is always more valuable because it allows the full $350 amount","The above-the-line deduction is more valuable because it reduces AGI and is available to all filers","Both deductions reduce taxable income identically with no strategic difference","The above-the-line deduction is less valuable because it phases out at lower income levels"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 62(a)(2)(D), the educator expense deduction is an above-the-line adjustment that reduces AGI. A lower AGI preserves eligibility for AGI-sensitive benefits such as education credits, the rental loss allowance under Section 469(i), and reduced Social Security taxability. The deduction is also available to taxpayers who claim the standard deduction.
Wrong (A): Although the itemized amount is larger ($350 vs. $300), it requires foregoing the standard deduction, which exceeds most educators'' total itemized deductions.
Wrong (C): Above-the-line and below-the-line deductions are not equivalent; above-the-line deductions reduce AGI, which affects many other tax provisions.
Wrong (D): The educator expense deduction under Section 62(a)(2)(D) does not phase out based on income level.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2652;

-- Q2694: Individual Taxation: Credits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Tuition paid directly to the educational institution","Required student activity fees paid to the institution","Course materials such as textbooks and required supplies","Laboratory fees charged by the educational institution"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 25A(f)(1)(A), the AOTC''s qualified expenses include tuition, fees, and course materials (textbooks, supplies, and equipment needed for enrollment). The Lifetime Learning Credit under Section 25A(f)(1)(B) covers only tuition and fees paid directly to the institution and does not include separately purchased course materials.
Wrong (A): Tuition qualifies for both the AOTC and the Lifetime Learning Credit, so it does not distinguish between the two.
Wrong (B): Student activity fees paid to the institution qualify for both credits and are not unique to the AOTC.
Wrong (D): Lab fees charged by the institution are included in qualifying fees for both credits and do not differentiate the AOTC.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2694;

-- Q2700: Individual Taxation: Credits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The child and dependent care credit only","The earned income credit only for qualifying filers","Neither the child and dependent care credit nor the EIC","Both credits without any limitation on amounts claimed"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 21(e)(4), married taxpayers filing separately are generally ineligible for the child and dependent care credit. IRC Section 32(d) similarly disallows the earned income credit for MFS filers. Both credits require a filing status of single, head of household, qualifying surviving spouse, or married filing jointly.
Wrong (A): The child and dependent care credit is denied to MFS filers under Section 21(e)(4), so it cannot be claimed alone.
Wrong (B): The earned income credit is completely disallowed for MFS filers under Section 32(d), so it cannot be claimed.
Wrong (D): Both credits are denied under MFS status; unlike the child tax credit, which remains available to MFS filers, these two credits are completely prohibited.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2700;

-- Q2703: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [easy/L3]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The phaseout has no effect because Porter''s AMTI is below the completion point","The exemption is reduced by $10,163, and the effective marginal rate rises to about 35%","The exemption is completely eliminated once AMTI exceeds $609,350 by any amount","The phaseout increases the exemption to reward taxpayers subject to AMT"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 55(d)(3), the AMT exemption is reduced by 25 cents for every dollar of AMTI exceeding $609,350. Porter''s $40,650 excess triggers a $10,163 reduction, leaving a $77,937 exemption. Each additional dollar also exposes $0.25 of previously exempt income to the 28% rate, creating an effective marginal rate of approximately 35%.
Wrong (A): Porter''s AMTI of $650,000 exceeds the $609,350 threshold, so the phaseout does apply and reduces the exemption.
Wrong (C): The exemption is not eliminated immediately upon exceeding the threshold; it is reduced gradually at 25 cents per dollar of excess.
Wrong (D): The phaseout reduces the exemption as AMTI increases; it does not reward higher-income taxpayers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2703;

-- Q2707: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Interest on U.S. Treasury bonds held in the portfolio","Interest on general obligation municipal bonds owned","Interest on private activity bonds issued after August 7, 1986","Interest on investment-grade corporate bonds in the portfolio"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
