-- Q2709: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["It is permanently lost and cannot offset future taxes","It generates an AMT credit to offset future regular tax liability","It is automatically refunded to Rachel by the IRS","It can only offset future AMT liability, not regular tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 53, when a taxpayer pays AMT due to timing or deferral items such as ISO exercises, the AMT paid generates a minimum tax credit (MTC) that carries forward indefinitely. This credit can offset regular tax liability in future years when the regular tax exceeds the tentative minimum tax.
Wrong (A): AMT paid on timing differences is not permanently lost; Section 53 provides a credit carryforward mechanism to prevent permanent double taxation.
Wrong (C): The AMT credit is not automatically refunded; it must be claimed on future returns when regular tax exceeds tentative minimum tax.
Wrong (D): The AMT credit specifically offsets regular tax liability, not future AMT; it reduces regular tax down to the tentative minimum tax amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2709;

-- Q2715: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$88,100, reflecting a partial reduction from the phaseout","$0, because the exemption is fully phased out at this level","$60,775, after applying the 25% reduction to the exemption","$88,100, the full exemption with no phaseout reduction applied"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 55(d), the AMT exemption for single filers is $88,100 for 2026, and the phaseout begins when AMTI exceeds approximately $609,350. Karen''s AMTI of $500,000 is below the threshold, so her full $88,100 exemption is available with no reduction.
Wrong (A): A partial reduction would apply only if Karen''s AMTI exceeded $609,350; at $500,000 the phaseout has not begun.
Wrong (B): The exemption is fully eliminated only when AMTI is high enough to exhaust the entire amount; $500,000 is well below that level.
Wrong (C): $60,775 would reflect an incorrect application of the phaseout formula; no reduction applies because Karen''s AMTI is below the threshold.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2715;

-- Q2716: Individual Taxation: Credits/AMT [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The credit expires permanently when the ISO stock is sold","The credit converts to a capital loss carryforward on the sale","The credit is added to the adjusted basis in the ISO stock","The credit offsets regular tax exceeding tentative minimum tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 53, the AMT credit from ISO timing preferences carries forward indefinitely and offsets regular tax liability to the extent regular tax exceeds tentative minimum tax. In the year of sale, the stock sale reverses the timing difference, typically causing regular tax to exceed AMT and allowing George to use the credit.
Wrong (A): The AMT credit does not expire upon sale of the stock; it carries forward indefinitely under Section 53 until fully utilized.
Wrong (B): The AMT credit is a tax credit, not a capital loss; it does not convert to a loss carryforward.
Wrong (C): The AMT credit is applied against tax liability on the return, not added to the basis of the underlying stock.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2716;

-- Q2739: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sandra, because she is the child''s biological mother","Neither parent unless they execute a written agreement","Whichever parent reports the higher adjusted gross income","The ex-husband, as the parent with greater custodial time"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 152(c)(4)(A), when a child qualifies under both parents'' households, the child is treated as the qualifying child of the parent with whom the child resided for the greater portion of the year. The son lived with the ex-husband 60% of the year, making him the custodial parent entitled to the claim.
Wrong (A): The mother does not receive automatic priority; the tiebreaker rule is based on residency time, not the parent''s gender or biological relationship.
Wrong (B): A written agreement is not required for the tiebreaker; the residency test under Section 152(c)(4)(A) determines the default claim, though Form 8332 can release it.
Wrong (C): Income is used as a secondary tiebreaker only when both parents have equal residency time; here the 60/40 split resolves the claim without considering income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2739;

-- Q2743: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because his spouse Nancy has no taxable income","Yes, if they lived apart the last 6 months with a dependent child","No, because married taxpayers are never eligible for this status","No, because head of household status requires being legally unmarried"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 7703(b), a married taxpayer may be "considered unmarried" and claim head of household status if they lived apart from their spouse for the last six months of the year and maintained a home for a qualifying child for more than half the year. Both conditions must be met.
Wrong (A): The spouse''s lack of income is irrelevant to head of household eligibility; the requirements focus on living arrangements and maintaining a household for a dependent.
Wrong (C): Married taxpayers can qualify for head of household if they meet the "considered unmarried" requirements under Section 7703(b).
Wrong (D): While head of household generally requires being unmarried, Section 7703(b) creates an exception for separated spouses meeting specific conditions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2743;

-- Q2753: Individual Taxation: Filing/Credits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Child tax credit if the qualifying child lives with them","Earned income credit based on their earned income amount","Full student loan interest deduction for qualifying payments","Child and dependent care credit for qualifying care expenses"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 24(a), a married filing separately taxpayer may claim the child tax credit for qualifying children who live with them. The phase-out begins at $200,000 AGI for MFS filers, compared to $400,000 for MFJ filers.
Wrong (B): The earned income credit is completely disallowed for MFS filers under IRC Section 32(d), regardless of income level.
Wrong (C): The student loan interest deduction under Section 221(e)(1) is not available to taxpayers filing as married filing separately.
Wrong (D): The child and dependent care credit is generally denied to MFS filers under IRC Section 21(e)(4).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 2753;
